WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.954 --> 00:00:26.447
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:10.513
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.669
Good evening, and welcome
to tonight's performance

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:40.950
by Gamelan Galak Tika.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:44.290
We hope that you enjoyed that
first selection, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.590
The next two pieces
will be performed

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:50.310
by a newly formed group as of
last year called Gamelan Anak

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:52.500
Tika or the Cambridge
Youth Gamelan.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.340
As you see here, they
are made up of children.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:58.560
Our group is in its
second year, and we

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.260
have students ranging from
third grade through ninth grade

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:02.760
although the group
that you see here

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:04.020
goes up to about sixth grade.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:07.590
And this is a community
gamelan program

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:11.010
that meets once a week
throughout the entire year

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.500
to teach children how to play
traditional Balinese music

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:15.993
and dance on the instruments
that you see before you.

00:09:15.993 --> 00:09:17.910
So the first piece that
we're going to perform

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.130
is a piece called "Gilak
Topeng [INAUDIBLE],","

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:24.150
which was taught to us by
our guest teacher from Bali

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.885
that visits the MIT group
every spring named Dewa Alit.

00:09:26.885 --> 00:09:29.010
It's a teaching piece, so
what you're going to hear

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:33.090
is the kids playing
a shorter melody

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.910
and listening to the drum
for cues on how to get loud

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:37.102
and how to get quiet.

00:09:37.102 --> 00:09:39.060
And the second piece is
a slightly longer piece

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.850
called "Baris" that is normally
performed with a male dancer

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:43.620
and is a very long
selection of music,

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.405
but we're playing about a
third of the music there

00:09:46.405 --> 00:09:47.280
and without a dancer.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:50.010
And in there, in that piece,
you're going to hear sort

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:53.518
of a flashy introduction
and responses to the drum

00:09:53.518 --> 00:09:55.560
in what's called an
[NON-ENGLISH] where they stop

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:58.320
their cycle of music rather
kind of abruptly and switch

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.900
to a second pattern and then go
back to the original pattern.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.138
So please welcome
onto the stage--

00:10:03.138 --> 00:10:05.430
and they're already here--
the Cambridge Youth Gamelan.

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:08.298
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:13.078 --> 00:10:16.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:13:12.917 --> 00:13:16.410
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:34.374 --> 00:13:37.368
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:01.750
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:15.250
[MUSIC - "BARIS"]

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:03.250
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:28.790
So while we're just setting up
here for the next three pieces

00:18:28.790 --> 00:18:31.130
before intermission,
I just wanted

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:33.170
to talk to you for a little bit.

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:34.100
So my name is Leslie.

00:18:34.100 --> 00:18:37.130
I've had the pleasure of working
with this extraordinary group

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:41.580
of musicians for the last
three and a little bit months.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:44.220
What you see when
Balinese gamelan happens,

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:46.260
everything that
happens on this stage

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:49.650
is learned by rote
with no written music,

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:51.840
with no instruments to
take home to practice on.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:54.570
These people come together
in a group of 25 people

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:59.262
and play and learn together,
so that's kind of awesome.

00:18:59.262 --> 00:18:59.970
You guys can sit.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.430
How many people are at their
first gamelan show ever?

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:06.930
Oh whoa, a lot.

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:07.620
Awesome.

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:10.740
OK, so I just want to give
you a little guided tour

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:12.240
of the ensemble so
that you can have

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:15.240
a sense of what to listen for.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:18.122
So when we play gamelan, a lot
of the music happens in cycles.

00:19:18.122 --> 00:19:20.580
You'll hear melodies that sort
of circle around and around,

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:23.040
and what is holding
that together

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:26.550
at the heart of the gamelan is
these hanging gongs at the back

00:19:26.550 --> 00:19:28.260
here.

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:29.440
They mark the cycle.

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:31.540
And this gong over
here-- will you wave?

00:19:31.540 --> 00:19:32.433
Thank you very much.

00:19:32.433 --> 00:19:34.350
It's called the kempli,
and it keeps the beat.

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:36.392
You might have noticed we
don't have a conductor,

00:19:36.392 --> 00:19:38.130
so there's a kempli
keeping the beat.

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:39.150
There are other
people who are sort

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:40.770
of controlling when
things get softer

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:43.717
and louder and
slower and faster.

00:19:43.717 --> 00:19:45.300
So what we're going
to do is basically

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:47.160
we're going to put
together one melody that

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.470
happens in the next piece that
we're going to play so that you

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:51.470
can hear sort of how all
the parts fit together,

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:53.738
and then you'll hear it in
the context of the piece.

00:19:53.738 --> 00:19:55.530
So the very first thing
I just want to hear

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:56.603
is the gong structures.

00:19:56.603 --> 00:19:58.020
It's an eight-beat
gong structure,

00:19:58.020 --> 00:19:59.312
and you're going to hear that--

00:19:59.312 --> 00:20:01.270
Ilya, will you just
hit the big gong?

00:20:01.270 --> 00:20:01.770
[GONG HIT]

00:20:01.770 --> 00:20:04.192
Yeah, that gong happens
at the end of every cycle.

00:20:04.192 --> 00:20:06.150
So when you hear that
gong, it means the melody

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:07.360
is coming around again.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:10.023
OK, go ahead.

00:20:10.023 --> 00:20:12.909
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:20:21.090 --> 00:20:21.962
Awesome.

00:20:21.962 --> 00:20:23.930
[GONG HIT]

00:20:23.930 --> 00:20:26.508
Overexcited.

00:20:26.508 --> 00:20:28.050
In the middle of
all of this is going

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:30.420
to be a melody that
moves every beat,

00:20:30.420 --> 00:20:35.126
and that's played by this
musician over here on the ugal.

00:20:35.126 --> 00:20:38.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:46.410
All of these musicians on this
side of the ensemble-- oh,

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:46.970
wait.

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:48.740
This instrument here does
a lot of different things.

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:50.060
You're going to notice
it does different things

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:50.893
in different pieces.

00:20:50.893 --> 00:20:53.090
In this particular
piece, secretly, it's

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:56.450
not usually used, but it's going
to be sort of mirroring what

00:20:56.450 --> 00:20:57.230
the ugal does.

00:20:57.230 --> 00:20:58.730
And these other
musicians back here

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:01.160
are going to be
playing sort of sparser

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:03.210
versions of that same
melody, so these guys here

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:05.210
are going to be playing
basically the same thing

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:08.012
that Sam is playing
over on the ugal.

00:21:08.012 --> 00:21:10.220
These guys are going to be
playing every second note.

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:12.428
These guys are going to be
playing every fourth note,

00:21:12.428 --> 00:21:15.170
so the more important notes get
emphasized by more instruments.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:20.120
So can we maybe just start
with Sam and Matt, and then one

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:22.370
cycle, one cycle, one
cycle, or 2, 2, 2, 2?

00:21:22.370 --> 00:21:24.161
Yeah.

00:21:24.161 --> 00:21:27.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:21:41.342 --> 00:21:42.300
These guys are sparser.

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:56.370
Thank you.

00:21:56.370 --> 00:22:00.000
Over on this side, these are
the fast, flowery melodies

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.250
that happen over top
of that, and they're

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:03.210
divided into partners.

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:05.460
Gamelan, especially
Balinese gamelan,

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.890
has this special
technique called kotekan,

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:09.572
which involves playing
a melody faster

00:22:09.572 --> 00:22:12.030
than a single person can play
it alone on their instrument,

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:13.440
and so that melody
becomes shared

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:16.170
between you and your partner.

00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:19.093
And so we're going to play
you the two parts separately,

00:22:19.093 --> 00:22:21.010
and then we'll play you
how they fit together.

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:23.220
So just a tiny bit
slower than that.

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:26.200
[NON-ENGLISH].

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:29.658
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:47.712
And [NON-ENGLISH].

00:22:47.712 --> 00:22:51.163
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:23:04.970 --> 00:23:08.878
And here's how the two
of them sound together.

00:23:08.878 --> 00:23:12.357
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:23:27.290 --> 00:23:29.180
Cool, huh?

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:31.490
The last instruments
that I want to talk about

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:33.260
play a little bit more
of a rhythmic role.

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:36.410
So over here, you'll see this
tiny little plate of cymbals.

00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:40.815
It's called ceng-ceng, and
that plays a sort of mostly

00:23:40.815 --> 00:23:43.190
chucka-chucka-chucka-chucka
kind of rhythmic role to hold

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:43.880
everybody together.

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:46.250
But you'll notice, he also does
some other fun things, too.

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:47.780
And I'm going to
abandon you for a minute

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:49.040
because I play drums on this.

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:54.800
The drums play fun
rhythms, but then also,

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:57.550
the drummer is the person who's
directing the gamelan through--

00:23:57.550 --> 00:23:59.300
Emeric, I think,
mentioned [NON-ENGLISH],,

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:00.800
when you get loud
and suddenly stop.

00:24:00.800 --> 00:24:03.260
Those sorts of things are
directed through the drums.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.300
When the tempo shifts,
when the dynamics shift,

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:07.680
this is all directed
through the drums as well.

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:10.190
So we'll just play you what
the drums and ceng-ceng

00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:12.590
sound like alone, and
then after that, we'll

00:24:12.590 --> 00:24:15.620
build it up from the slowest
instruments into the fastest

00:24:15.620 --> 00:24:18.200
instruments so that you can
hear what this melody sounds

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:19.890
like all built up.

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:21.000
Cool?

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:21.876
Excellent.

00:24:39.732 --> 00:24:43.204
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:25:00.564 --> 00:25:01.914
That's how that works.

00:25:01.914 --> 00:25:03.956
And so now we'll build it
up, two cycles of each,

00:25:03.956 --> 00:25:06.734
starting from gongs and the next
sparsest instrument all the way

00:25:06.734 --> 00:25:08.842
up. [? That's how ?]
[INAUDIBLE] coming together.

00:25:11.818 --> 00:25:15.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:09.820
So see if you can hear
that melody in this piece

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:11.410
that we're going
to play for you.

00:26:11.410 --> 00:26:13.240
So we'll play this
piece, and then directly

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:15.490
after, we're going to play
the piece that was actually

00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:18.707
composed by one of our
members, Sam, so [INAUDIBLE]..

00:26:33.677 --> 00:26:37.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:38:00.251 --> 00:38:03.744
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:09.612 --> 00:39:13.105
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:44:10.030 --> 00:44:13.509
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:40.940 --> 00:44:44.300
We have one more
piece, and then there

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:47.780
will be a short intermission
while we move instruments.

00:44:47.780 --> 00:44:50.460
And after the
intermission, we'll

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:55.290
be in an entirely different
gamelan with a piano soloist,

00:44:55.290 --> 00:44:57.690
so please stick around for that.

00:44:57.690 --> 00:44:59.760
And during the
intermission, there

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:04.740
will be some merchandise in
the lobby, CDs, t-shirts, PEZ

00:45:04.740 --> 00:45:06.390
dispensers, things like that.

00:45:37.531 --> 00:45:40.525
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:55:26.757 --> 00:55:30.749
[APPLAUSE]

00:56:00.720 --> 00:56:06.120
This particular project is
the culmination of something

00:56:06.120 --> 00:56:08.160
that we started this
summer when we realized

00:56:08.160 --> 00:56:12.990
that incredible influence and
person of the 20th century, Lou

00:56:12.990 --> 00:56:17.140
Harrison, composer Lou Harrison,
that this was his centennial.

00:56:17.140 --> 00:56:19.710
And we also realized
that we had one

00:56:19.710 --> 00:56:23.250
of his great champions and
also, in fact, his teacher

00:56:23.250 --> 00:56:27.000
of Javanese music, Jody
Diamond, in the area

00:56:27.000 --> 00:56:31.590
along with the instruments
that he and his partner William

00:56:31.590 --> 00:56:33.010
Colvig built.

00:56:33.010 --> 00:56:35.010
So these instruments that
you're seeing on stage

00:56:35.010 --> 00:56:39.150
were actually built by
hand by Lou and Bill

00:56:39.150 --> 00:56:41.280
and named after their
patron, Betty Freeman,

00:56:41.280 --> 00:56:43.950
and they are Gamelan Si Betty.

00:56:43.950 --> 00:56:46.110
They are in the style
of a Javanese Gamelan,

00:56:46.110 --> 00:56:50.580
although they have some touches
that are uniquely Lou and Bill.

00:56:50.580 --> 00:56:55.950
The tuning was picked out
by Lou in collaboration

00:56:55.950 --> 00:56:59.460
with his other Javanese
teacher, the master Pak Cokro,

00:56:59.460 --> 00:57:02.910
and the designs are from
a Pennsylvania Dutch floor

00:57:02.910 --> 00:57:07.680
pattern that Lou and
Bill painted themselves.

00:57:07.680 --> 00:57:11.160
To do this piece-- this is a
piece he wrote in the 1980s

00:57:11.160 --> 00:57:13.140
for his friend,
Belle Bullwinkle,

00:57:13.140 --> 00:57:16.590
and his own gamelan,
which Jody was a part of.

00:57:16.590 --> 00:57:18.850
And she has been
incredibly generous,

00:57:18.850 --> 00:57:21.720
teaching us to play
traditional Javanese style

00:57:21.720 --> 00:57:25.380
and then teaching us how to do
Lou Harrison's take on Javanese

00:57:25.380 --> 00:57:26.280
style for this piece.

00:57:26.280 --> 00:57:27.510
It's in three movements.

00:57:27.510 --> 00:57:31.922
It both follows Javanese form,
which is similar to the forms

00:57:31.922 --> 00:57:33.630
Leslie was showing
you in the first half,

00:57:33.630 --> 00:57:39.000
but it has his own unique
sense of melody and drama.

00:57:39.000 --> 00:57:42.540
We did this two
months ago at the ICA.

00:57:42.540 --> 00:57:44.310
Were any of you there for that?

00:57:44.310 --> 00:57:46.920
All right, well, so
I don't say anymore.

00:57:46.920 --> 00:57:48.810
Thank you for coming again.

00:57:48.810 --> 00:57:50.880
So we played with an
incredible pianist.

00:57:50.880 --> 00:57:53.880
Sarah Cahill sends her regards.

00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:56.610
She's in Japan right now,
playing Lou Harrison's music

00:57:56.610 --> 00:57:57.250
there.

00:57:57.250 --> 00:58:01.890
But the thing is that there's
such a depth of talent

00:58:01.890 --> 00:58:05.620
in this ensemble that
while she was away,

00:58:05.620 --> 00:58:09.180
I just kind of asked one of
the members, Nick Joliat,

00:58:09.180 --> 00:58:11.460
if he could just kind of sub
for her on the piano part

00:58:11.460 --> 00:58:13.770
during rehearsals.

00:58:13.770 --> 00:58:15.870
So Sarah's amazing, and
she sounded incredible.

00:58:15.870 --> 00:58:19.090
But he sounded so good that
we just thought, why don't we

00:58:19.090 --> 00:58:20.250
just do it again with Nick?

00:58:20.250 --> 00:58:21.550
He's in the neighborhood.

00:58:21.550 --> 00:58:25.887
And so he is there at
the piano, and we're just

00:58:25.887 --> 00:58:26.970
thrilled to play with him.

00:58:26.970 --> 00:58:28.530
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:28.530 --> 00:58:34.690
This is the end of the
residency of Si Betty at MIT

00:58:34.690 --> 00:58:38.190
and Jody's residency with
us, and so we're a little bit

00:58:38.190 --> 00:58:39.340
sentimental about that.

00:58:39.340 --> 00:58:42.367
But hopefully, it's just the
end of this incarnation of it,

00:58:42.367 --> 00:58:43.950
and we'll have her
and the instruments

00:58:43.950 --> 00:58:45.970
back again in the future.

00:58:45.970 --> 00:58:48.330
In any case, it's
just been incredibly

00:58:48.330 --> 00:58:51.600
enriching to play this
music, to work with her,

00:58:51.600 --> 00:58:52.830
to work with the ensemble.

00:58:52.830 --> 00:58:54.420
So that's enough said.

00:58:54.420 --> 00:58:57.510
This is the Lou Harrison
"Concerto for Piano

00:58:57.510 --> 00:59:00.654
with Javanese Gamelan."

00:59:00.654 --> 00:59:04.227
[MUSIC - LOU HARRISON, "CONCERTO
FOR PIANO WITH JAVANESE

00:59:04.227 --> 00:59:55.030
GAMELAN"]

01:08:49.330 --> 01:08:51.130
[APPLAUSE]

01:09:07.930 --> 01:09:10.030
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:22:18.730 --> 01:22:22.080
[APPLAUSE]

